/-!
# Degree-zero principal divisors over K(x), characteristic 0

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.hasPrincipalDivisors_of_transcendental`
(`b5b9418c-8f88-532c-ad06-cece77886a29`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `602fd9f3-cfb9-5676-a6f8-f9318735d23c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field of characteristic $0$, let $F$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure,
and let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$. Assume that $F$ is finite-dimensional as a vector
space over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}`. The conclusion is the
class `HasPrincipalDivisors K F`, i.e. the assertion that for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there
exists a divisor $D$ — a finitely supported function from the places of $F$ over $K$ to
$\mathbb{Z}$, a place being a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F$ which contains
$\operatorname{im}(K \to F)$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring — such that $D(v) =
v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ for every place $v$, and such that $D$ has degree $0$, the degree being the sum
$\sum_v D(v) \cdot v.\mathrm{deg}$ over the (finite) support of $D$ of the values of $D$ weighted by
the integers $v.\mathrm{deg}$ attached to the places. In particular the existence of such a finitely
supported $D$ encodes that $v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ vanishes for all but finitely many $v$.

This is the degree-zero theorem for principal divisors on an algebraic function field of one
variable, here in the characteristic-zero case and in the form in which the field is presented as a
finite extension of a rational subfield $K(x)$ generated by a transcendental element; the classical
statement holds for arbitrary algebraic function fields of one variable, and no such generality is
claimed here. It supplies the `HasPrincipalDivisors` hypothesis used elsewhere in the development,
for instance in the variant for fields generated over $K$ by a transcendental element and in the
identity for the sum of the quantities $e_v - 1$ over places.

